00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:39.540
But anyways, taking a look back, the story
of Sony’s fall from grace can be attributed

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:44.940
to a lot of factors but likely one of the
most consistent factors is repeatedly missing

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.060
the boat with new technology.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:53.690
For example, let’s take a look back to October
23, 2001 right after the dotcom bubble burst.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:57.900
Most tech companies were reeling from their
dotcom losses and struggling to secure funding

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.409
and improve employee morale.

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:02.420
But, there was one notable exception: Apple.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:07.989
Apple had already gone through their own existential
crisis in the mid 90s and they were just now

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:13.670
starting to turn things around and October
23, 2001 was the fateful day that they would

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.390
announce the iPod.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:20.329
In retrospect, this doesn't seem like a big
deal especially in comparison to the iPhone,

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:24.120
but this was actually a massive blow for Sony.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:30.410
You see, eversince the Walkman launched in
1979, Sony became synonymous with portable

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:31.410
audio.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:36.210
Just to give you sense of how successful the
Walkman was, Sony would go on to sell over

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.190
400 million units.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:44.660
But, with a single presentation, Apple would
throw two decades of precedent out the window

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:48.070
and establish themselves as the modern music
player.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:53.450
Fast forward 5 years to March 11, 2005, and
Sony would find themselves in a very similar

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:54.580
situation.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:59.960
Samsung would announce the world’s largest
LCD TV coming in at 82 inches.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:05.699
This TV was completely out of reach for the
average person but that didn’t matter.

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:09.979
This TV wasn’t designed to sell a bunch
of units or even push forward technology.

00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:14.150
It was designed to make people think that
Samsung was pushing forward technology.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:17.509
It established an association between Samsung
and innovation.

00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:21.319
And this was just the first of many Samsung
theatrics.

00:05:21.319 --> 00:05:28.690
Then you would have 3d TVs, curved TVs, OLED,
QLED, miniLED, nanoLED, and whatever else

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.240
they decide to slap before LED.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:36.310
To be honest, most consumers have no clue
what any of these terms actually mean.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.460
But, that doesn't matter.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.569
All that matters is that people associate
all these new fancy terms with Samsung and

00:05:43.569 --> 00:05:45.530
LG, not Sony.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:49.860
And that’s not a coincidence, that’s by
design.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.889
Basically the same thing played out within
the digital camera space as well.

00:05:53.889 --> 00:05:57.900
Up until the 2000s, Kodak was an untouchable
behemoth.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:02.979
They controlled over 80% of the US market
and about half of the global film market.

00:06:02.979 --> 00:06:07.400
Not to mention, they were the 5th most valuable
brand in the world.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.520
But, they would eventually drop the ball with
digital cameras making way for new competitors

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:13.520
to rise up.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.880
This would’ve been the perfect time for
Sony to brand themselves as the leader of

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.080
digital cameras.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:24.260
But, instead Canon would take on this image
and become the new dominant player.

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:28.830
None of this is to say that Sony was truly
behind in any of these markets whether it’s

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.020
mp3 players, TVs, or cameras.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:36.080
In fact, they would usually launch similar
products in parallel with the market leaders.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.430
And if you ask anyone who actually bought
Sony products, they would have nothing but

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:42.280
good things to say about Sony.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:47.120
In fact, many experts would say that Sony
products are more reliable and higher quality

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.170
than these so called market leaders to this
day.

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:54.860
But, none of this matters because the reality
was that their competitors destroyed them

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:57.480
when it came to brand and market positioning.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.520
And it wasn’t just one or two segments,
it was across the board.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.889
But this only tells half the story as not
all of Sony’s decline can just be explained

00:07:05.889 --> 00:07:10.660
away by just strong competition.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:19.509
SONY DROPS THE BALL:
The reality is that much of Sony’s demise

00:07:19.509 --> 00:07:21.370
is actually their own fault.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:25.419
You see, Sony was very much bloated heading
into the 2000s.

00:07:25.419 --> 00:07:29.539
They had just come off of an incredible run
throughout the 80s and 90s which led to them

00:07:29.539 --> 00:07:32.690
expanding into every industry you can think
of.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:37.199
Sony had a bank, an insurance division, they
were making anime, they were pushing out James

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:39.910
Bond movies, they were all over the place.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:44.080
The reason that Sony was so successful in
the first place was because they were able

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.419
to produce extremely reliable and affordable
consumer electronics.

00:07:48.419 --> 00:07:53.080
But as they expanded into every sector under
the sun, not only did they start to lose this

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:58.069
identity but they were spending more and more
time just managing all of these businesses

00:07:58.069 --> 00:08:01.099
as opposed to running and improving them.

00:08:01.099 --> 00:08:05.690
It was the classic pitfalls of a bloated company
with nearly 200,000 employees.

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:11.580
But, likely the best evidence that Sony had
lost their way was their executive team.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.539
For some reason, whenever companies get really
big, they decide to hire random outsiders

00:08:16.539 --> 00:08:21.590
as CEOs as opposed to someone who’s been
there and knows the company in and out.

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:27.190
Intel has done this, GE has done this, Nokia
has done this, and Sony would do this as well.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:32.219
In the late 90s, Sony would hire a lifelong
media executive named Howard Stringer to be

00:08:32.219 --> 00:08:34.539
the president of US operations.

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:40.560
I’ve got nothing personal against Howard,
but seriously, this guy didn’t have an engineering

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.630
background, had no experience running an electronics
company, and had little grasp of what made

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:46.990
Sony successful in the first place.

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:52.170
But for whatever reason, Sony would decide
to prompt Howard to CEO and Chairman of Sony

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:53.170
in 2005.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:58.760
Also, let me note, he couldn’t even speak
Japanese which you would think is quite important

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:02.660
for a Japanese company with a majority of
employees being Japanese.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:06.329
I mean, forget being able to talk with the
average employee.

00:09:06.329 --> 00:09:10.630
How was Howard supposed to communicate with
his executive team who were almost all Japanese.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:16.510
I’m sure they could communicate in English
but I think you can see why this isn’t exactly

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:17.920
an ideal setup.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:23.090
Yeah, I don’t think you’d be surprised
to hear that Sony stock would fall 75% during

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:24.660
his tenure.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.899
Likely the only notable thing that Howard
accomplished during his tenure was invest

00:09:27.899 --> 00:09:32.360
in Spotify which to his credit made Sony nearly
a billion dollars.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:36.899
But aside from this, Howard was more or less
just a dud.

00:09:36.899 --> 00:09:41.829
But while, Howard definitely did make things
any better at Sony, it wasn’t all his fault

00:09:41.829 --> 00:09:45.470
as Sony’s original secret sauce wouldn’t
have played out all that well in the first

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:46.470
place.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:51.170
As we mentioned, Sony had built their entire
company and brand around the idea of making

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:56.550
great reliable products which is exactly what
people were looking for the 80s and 90s.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:01.890
But, moving into the 2000s, consumer demand
very much shifted from wanting great products

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:04.660
to wanting great experiences.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:08.070
This seems like a novel difference but it’s
not.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:11.589
People became less concerned about the spec
sheet and the price.

00:10:11.589 --> 00:10:16.320
They were more concerned about how the product
made them feel and how it fit into their lives.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.000
This is exactly what Apple capitalized on
to great success.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.940
So, even if Sony did stick to their roots
and didn’t get distracted by other businesses

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:31.110
and an American CEO, it probably wouldn’t
have played out all that well anyway.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:35.890
This is basically what they tried doing with
the smartphone industry and it clearly didn’t

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:36.890
work.

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:42.380
But truth be told, it’s not all Sony’s
fault.

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:49.589
MARKET HEADWINDS:
Sony was very much subject to the headwinds

00:10:49.589 --> 00:10:51.779
of tech obsolescence.

00:10:51.779 --> 00:10:56.570
Much of what Sony was and is known for, people
just don’t buy anymore.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:58.089
Take cameras for example.

00:10:58.089 --> 00:11:01.100
Back in the day, everyone used to have a home
camera.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:05.990
But, with the rise of smartphones, there’s
literally no reason for a dedicated camera

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:10.240
unless you’re extremely passionate about
photography and film or it’s your job.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:13.399
The same thing can be said about mp3 players.

00:11:13.399 --> 00:11:17.950
No one has a dedicated device to listen to
music nowadays, they just use their phones.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:22.510
So, even if Sony stayed relevant after the
iPod came out, they would’ve just gotten

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:24.130
crushed by the iPhone anyway.

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:27.829
It’s a similar story with home entertainment
as well.

00:11:27.829 --> 00:11:31.040
People no longer use BluRay players or CDs
at all.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.430
They just use a streaming service and TV and
even that’s on the decline.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:41.149
And speaking of TVs, theses are still extremely
popular but they’re also extremely unviable

00:11:41.149 --> 00:11:42.300
as a business.

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:45.260
The TV industry has very much become a race
to the bottom.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:51.079
In fact, TVs have been regularly dropping
15 to 20% in price every year for the past

00:11:51.079 --> 00:11:52.570
20 years.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:57.760
At this point, for Samsung and LG, TVs are
just way to maintain brand presence and dominance,

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.250
not a way to make profit.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:04.340
This strategy works because they have plenty
of other industries to rely on.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:07.480
The same, however, cannot be said about Sony.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:11.680
The bottomline for them is that most of their
key industries have either become completely

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:17.449
obsolete or insanely commoditized both of
which are terrible for business.

00:12:17.449 --> 00:12:21.990
This is why Sony was losing billions every
year and heading towards an ugly end for much

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:23.389
of the 2010s.

00:12:23.389 --> 00:12:27.630
But, things didn’t eventually turn around,
so what happened?

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:32.110
Well, I wish I could say that Sony finally
figured out a new winning strategy and that

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:37.510
their making the comeback of the century but
that’s not really what happened.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:41.280
All that happened was that Sony basically
got their act together.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:47.220
In 2012, they put a Sony veteran in charge,
Kazuo Hirai and he basically just salvaged

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:49.029
the remaining parts of Sony.

00:12:49.029 --> 00:12:53.440
He shut down their unprofitable sectors, he
reduced their workforce from 170,000 to just

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:58.040
over a 100,000, and he spun off their key
sectors into their own businesses.

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:03.300
So, essentially, he separated out the businesses
that were still salvageable, made them independent

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:06.889
of the bloated giant, and cut everything else.

00:13:06.889 --> 00:13:12.019
This has allowed Sony to return to profitability
and recover much of their stock losses but

00:13:12.019 --> 00:13:16.550
make no mistake, Sony is still just a shell
their former selves.

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:22.240
In fact, their biggest revenue driver after
the PS5 is not even an electronics division.

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:25.550
It’s actually their background financial
services division.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:31.389
So, the glory days of Sony are very much behind
us which is quite a shame given how great

00:13:31.389 --> 00:13:33.390
their products really are.

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:36.980
The silver lining, however, is that while
Sony isn’t making a massive electronics

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:41.949
comeback, they have figured out a way to achieve
profitability with what they still have.

00:13:41.949 --> 00:13:46.329
And that’s a lot better than their Japanese
peers.

00:13:46.329 --> 00:13:49.930
Speaking of Japanese peers, Panasonic also
isn’t doing all that great.

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:54.810
Check out this video to learn more.

